# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The cooperative cancellation pattern via `CancellationToken` is the replacement.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Wait will throw OperationCanceledException if the token fires
            // before the task starts, or TimeoutException if we handle it ourselves.
            if (task.Wait(timeout))
                return task.Result;

            cts.Cancel();
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerException is not null)
        {
            // Unwrap single inner exceptions for cleaner call-site handling
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerExceptions);
        }
    }

    // Preferred async overload — pass the token INTO the work
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work` ignores the token, it keeps running. |
| **Task.Run + Wait can deadlock** | In UI/ASP.NET contexts with a `SynchronizationContext`, prefer the async overload. |
| **Resource leaks** | The old code killed the thread mid-flight. Now the work may complete *after* timeout — ensure `work` cleans up on cancellation. |
| **`default(T)` race** | The original code had a subtle race: `result` could be read before the thread wrote it. `Task<T>` eliminates this. |

## Making Existing Work Cancellable

The hard part: your `Func<T>` must check the token to actually stop:

```csharp
// Before (uninterruptible)
Func<int> work = () => ExpensiveComputation();

// After (cooperative)
Func<CancellationToken, Task<int>> work = async (ct) =>
{
    foreach (var chunk in data)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // check periodically
        Process(chunk);
    }
    return result;
};
```

For truly uncooperative code (e.g., blocking third-party calls), the last resort is running in a separate **process** — not thread — and killing that process on timeout.
